  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nThe core idea is to incorporate physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) and physics-based optimization to model the step function `h(x)` as a solution to a differential equation that minimizes overlap. By representing `h(x)` as a solution to a PDE that optimizes its shape for minimal self-overlap, we leverage physics-guidance to escape local optima more effectively than traditional heuristic methods. This approach differs from coordinate descent by learning the underlying structure of the optimal step function and then optimizing its parameters. The expected outcome is a lower Erd\u0151s constant by `C_5` due to better generalization in the solution space.\n\nThe primary bottleneck addressed is the trade-off between local search efficiency and global optimality in the high-dimensional search space. We argue that a physically-based optimization will naturally incorporate scale-dependent features, while avoiding the grid-dependence and suboptimal features of current methods.\n\n2. Mathematical specification  \nWe formulate the optimization problem as a system of PDEs that `h(x)` should satisfy:  \n- `\u2202/\u2202x (h(1-h)) = 0` (local balance between active and inactive states)  \n- Boundary conditions: `h(0) = 0`, `h(2) = 1` (activation across domain)  \n- Constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = 1  \n\nDiscretization and PINN Representation:  \nUse 400 grid points to represent `h(x)` as a neural network solution that satisfies the PDE on a residual-learning basis. Define:  \n- Spatial domain: `[0, 2]` with `dx = 2/400`  \n- Temporal domain for training: `t \u2208 [0, T]` (internal training parameter)  \n- Optimization variable: a low-dimensional set of initial conditions and neural-network weights  \n\nObjective: minimize `C\u2085` translation to the PDE framework:  \n`L(h) = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx` must satisfy the PDE and boundary conditions.  \n\nHyperparameters:  \n- Training loss: combination of PINN residuals (PDE, BCs) and objective `C\u2085`  \n- Number of hidden layers: 3  \n- Neurons per layer: 20  \n- Adam optimizer with adaptive learning rate  \n\nConstraint Enforcement: Use Lagrange multipliers or penalty terms in the loss function to enforce \u222bh(x)dx = 1.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   a. Preprocessing:\n      - Convert initial_grid from `initial_h_values` to a 400-point grid with `h[0]=0, h[2]=1`\n      - Define PDE residual function and boundary conditions\n\n   b. PINN Architecture:\n      ```python\n      import torch\n      import torch.nn as nn\n\n      class PhysicsNet(nn.Module):\n          def __init__(self):\n              super().__init__()\n              hidden_layers = []\n              for i in range(3):\n                  hidden_layers.append(nn.Linear(2, 20))\n                  hidden_layers.append(nn.ReLU())\n              hidden_layers.append(nn.Linear(20, 1))\n              self.model = nn.Sequential(*hidden_layers)\n\n          def forward(self, x):\n              # x is shape (batch, 2) where x[:,0] is x-coordinate, x[:,1] is time\n              return self.model(x)\n\n      def pde_loss(model, x, dx=2/400):\n          # Compute spatial derivatives using central differences\n          ...\n      ```\n\n   c. Optimization loop:\n      - Initialize PINN parameters randomly\n      - Compute initial objective via discretization\n      - Train PINN using physics-informed loss (PDE + objective + constraints)\n      - Use LBFGS for few steps (direct solver) to escape shallow minima\n\n   d. Global optimization outer-loop:\n      - Use Bayesian optimization with tree-of-perturbations\n      - Continuously monitor C\u2085 value and physics enforcement\n      - Early termination if gradient-based training fails\n\n   e. Final validation:\n      - Rebuild `h` from developed PINN solution\n      - Re-discretize and compute C\u2085 independently\n      - Verify integral and boundary conditions\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - Architecture variations: change hidden layers to 2 or 4; vary neurons [10,15,...,30]  \n   - Physics fidelity: include activation equations (HH model for neuroscience inspired)  \n   - Optimization methods: Adam, LBFGS, or SGD with momentum  \n   - Random restart variation: initialize from different boundary conditions  \n\n5. Budget and robustness\n   - Training time: approx. 800s for baseline training  \n   - Use 30% for validation phases  \n   - Adaptive batch size: start small (Rams\u00e9y growth)  \n   - C\u2085 evaluation: one estimate per trial  \n\n6. Return contract\n   - Compute constraints explicitly using integrals  \n   - Return `h` via discretized solution from PINN  \n   - Include leading 20 iterations for monitoring",
